 |rtx.atmosphere.nubis3InteriorTexture|float|0|||Nubis3: interior density texture strength \[0\.\.1\]\. Modulates the density INSIDE the body by the raw detail noise \(the stand\-in for Nubis3's authored per\-voxel Density Scale NVDF and iw3xo's multiplicative self\-gate\), so lit cloud faces show billow\-scale light variation instead of saturating to a flat white mass\. 0 = flat interiors \(old behavior\)\. Applies live\.|
 |rtx.atmosphere.nubis3ShapeVarietyKm|float|1.11|||Nubis3: mid\-frequency SHAPE displacement amplitude in km \[0\.\.1\.5\] \(the GT7 mid\-band role\)\. Pushes/pulls the body iso\-surface by up to half this at ~2\.4 km wavelengths — lobes, notches and full splits that turn round singular blobs into varied cloud clusters\. Whole\-body reshaping, not edge detail; coverage\-neutral on average\. 0 = off\. Applies live, no rebake\.|
 |rtx.atmosphere.nubis3SharpenStrength|float|1|||Nubis3: blend toward the paper's pow\(\) density sharpen \[0\.\.1\], which lifts low densities to bring out definition in wisps and edges\. 0 = off \(raw erosion output\)\. Applies live\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspective|bool|True|||Draws the air between the camera and every surface: haze, fog, sun shafts and the glow around lamps\. Near the camera it uses a scene\-shadowed froxel grid, and beyond that an unshadowed far\-field table\. This is the strongest distance cue in an outdoor scene\. With fog and local lights on, it can replace the renderer's own volumetric fog\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveDebugView|int|0|0|10|Haze debug view: replaces each pixel with one measurement of the haze, to find where its light comes from\. A strip along the bottom of the screen shows the scale, so compare a pixel with the strip rather than with how bright it looks\. 0: Off\. 1: Sun Visibility at the pixel's depth \(gray, white = sees the sun; small values below 0\.1 show in red, stretched ten times, so a small leak is not mistaken for none\)\. 2: Sky Visibility at the pixel's depth\. 3: Haze Brightness, the brightness the haze adds at the pixel in cd/m^2, in false color from 0\.0001 \(dark blue\) to 10000 \(white\) with a dark line at every power of ten\. 4: No Sun or Sky Light, the normal image with the near\-field haze lit as if nothing saw the sun or the sky\. 5: Haze Transmittance \(white = clear\)\. 6: Sun Visibility 2 m from the camera\. 7: Sky Visibility 2 m from the camera\. 8: Haze Brightness with no sun or sky light \(3 and 4 together\)\. 9: Surface Clamp, the share of the haze samples moved in front of the surface the game's depth shows \(white = all\)\. 10: Surface Guard, the share moved further in front because the game's depth lay behind the visible geometry, as with displaced surfaces, mirrors and glass\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveDisagreementWeight|float|0.25|0|1|How fast the near\-field haze lets go of a fully lit or fully shadowed history once the scene says otherwise: an occluder moving through a sun shaft, a door, or a jump in the time of day\. When a froxel's ray contradicts such a history and two more rays confirm it, the new value gets this blend weight instead of the slow history one\. Higher reacts faster but lets a rare stray ray show as a flicker\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveEdgeHistoryMargin|float|0.15|0|0.5|When the camera turns, the near\-field froxels that come into view at the screen edge have no history\. Those whose last\-frame position lies within this share of the screen outside the old view start from the nearest edge froxels' history instead of from scratch, and settle within a few frames \(Reveal History Frames\)\. 0\.15 is 15% of the screen\. Higher covers faster turns, but the borrowed values are less likely to match\. 0 makes them start fresh\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveFastReveal|bool|True|||Air that a wall or object hid last frame and that is in view now \(a door opening, the camera stepping past a corner, a vehicle driving off\) re\-converges within Reveal History Frames\. While hidden, such air only holds a copy of the air in front of the surface, and without this it fades out over the full history, which shows as a lingering ghost of the object in the haze\. Off keeps the full history age\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveFroxelDepthSlices|int|96|16|256|The number of depth slices in the near\-field haze, spread from the camera to Froxel Range\. More slices give finer detail in depth\. GPU cost and memory grow linearly with it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveFroxelRangeMeters|float|1000|10|50000|How far the near\-field haze reaches from the camera, in meters\. Inside it the air is shadowed by the scene \(sun shafts, dark interiors\), lit by local lights and carries the height fog\. Beyond it, an unshadowed far field carries the atmosphere to the horizon\. Raising it spreads the same slices over a longer distance\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveFroxelSizePixels|int|8|2|64|The screen size of one froxel of the near\-field haze, in render\-resolution pixels\. 8 gives a 240x135 grid at 1080p\. Smaller froxels sharpen sun shafts and lamp glow\. GPU cost and memory grow with 1 / size squared\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveHashedTurnPhase|bool|True|||Each near\-field froxel alternates its ray between the sun and the sky\. With this on, every froxel starts the alternation on its own scrambled frame instead of a fixed checkerboard\. While the camera moves, air that shifts an odd whole number of froxels a frame no longer gets stuck seeing only the sun or only the sky, which showed as blotches in the haze in motion\. Off keeps the checkerboard\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveHistoryFrames|int|32|1|255|How many frames of history each near\-field froxel averages\. Each frame a froxel traces one sun\-or\-sky visibility ray and one local\-light ray, and the history smooths them into stable shadowing\. More frames look smoother, but a moving light or occluder takes longer to settle\. Camera cuts reset the history\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightCutoff|float|0.002|0||The glow brightness below which a light counts as not reaching a point\. Each light's reach is sized from its own power, so a torch stops after a few meters and a floodlight reaches much further\. Lower it if bright lights visibly stop lighting the fog at a fixed distance\. Raise it to save GPU time\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightFilterRadius|int|1|0|2|Averages how much each froxel sees of its local lights with its neighbors on screen, this many froxels to each side, before lamps glow through it\. Each froxel tests one light per frame, so in dark scenes lit by lamps the shadowing of the glow is grainy without it\. Larger values are smoother but soften the shadows that objects cast into a lamp's glow\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightIntensity|float|1|0|100|A gain on the glow of local lights in the haze\. 1 is physical\. Raise it when a game's lights are authored dimmer than the haze that looks right at a distance\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightMaxCount|int|256|0|4096|The maximum number of scene lights the near\-field haze uses per frame\. The lights that can glow brightest in view are kept\. Lower it to save GPU time in scenes with very many lights\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightShadows|bool|True|||Traces a shadow ray from each froxel toward the local light it sampled\. Without it, a lamp lights the fog on both sides of the wall it stands behind\. With it, light through a doorway casts a real shaft\. Needs ray\-traced scene geometry\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightTileMaxCount|int|64|1|64|The most lights whose glow any 64x64\-pixel screen tile draws \(8x8 froxels at the default froxel size\)\. The brightest lights are kept\. When more lights than this reach a tile, the dimmer ones are left out there and pop at the tile's border\. Each light in a tile costs every pixel of the tile a few taps, so lower it only to save GPU time in scenes packed with lamps\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLightWindow|bool|False|||Fades each local light's glow smoothly to zero at its cull radius, instead of letting it fall off with distance and stop abruptly at that radius\. Turn it on for games whose lights are authored with such a falloff window\. Local Light Cutoff then sets the reach\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveLocalLights|bool|True|||Lets scene lights such as lamps, torches, muzzle flashes and headlights glow in the near\-field haze, alongside the sun and sky\. The cost follows how many lights are near each other on screen, not the total light count\. With this on, the renderer's own volumetric lighting can be turned off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveMieAnisotropyMax|float|0.8|-1|1|The upper limit on how strongly the haze scatters light forward, used by the haze in front of surfaces but not by the sky\. A strongly forward\-scattering haze is brightest looking toward the sun, where the far field cannot know that the air is in shadow\. Lower it if the haze glows too much toward a sun hidden behind scenery\. Values at or above Mie Anisotropy have no effect\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectivePenumbraEveryFrame|bool|True|||Near\-field froxels that are partly shadowed from the sun, or see part of the sky \(between 5% and 95%\), trace that ray every frame instead of every other frame\. That is where a shadow estimate is noisiest, so shaft edges, doorways and canopies settle about twice as fast and grain less\. It costs extra rays only in those froxels\. Off keeps strict alternation\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveRevealHistoryFrames|int|4|0|255|The history length, in frames, given to near\-field froxels whose history is only an approximation: froxels that came into view at the screen edge, and air that a wall or object hid last frame \(Fast Reveal\)\. They re\-converge within about this many frames instead of over the full history\. Lower adapts faster but is noisier for those frames\. 0 gives them their full history age\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveSceneShadow|bool|True|||Traces the scene to find how much of the sun and the sky each near\-field froxel sees\. This gives sun shafts through doorways and foliage, and keeps interiors from filling with sunlit haze\. Needs ray\-traced scene geometry\. Without it the near field is unshadowed\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveSilhouetteFix|bool|True|||Stops dark halos in the haze around objects seen against the sky or a farther background\. A froxel sample that lands on a nearer object is moved in front of it, where the object blocks part of the sky and sun, and blended into froxels that also stand for the open air beside and behind the object\. With this on, such samples leave those froxels alone and only samples that see past the object update them\. Air behind dense foliage cards updates a little more slowly\. Off blends every sample\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveSkipHiddenFroxels|bool|True|||Near\-field froxels that lie behind the visible surfaces of every nearby screen tile trace no rays: nothing on screen can see them, so they copy the air in front of those surfaces from the history\. Indoors this is most of the grid past the first walls, so it saves most of the haze's rays there\. When such air comes into view, it re\-converges within Reveal History Frames\. Off traces every froxel\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveSkyEveryFrameWithoutSun|bool|True|||While the sun is below the horizon, each near\-field froxel traces its sky ray every frame instead of every other frame \(the skipped sun ray's turn goes to the sky\)\. The sky light in dark, hazy scenes then settles twice as fast and is less noisy, for no extra rays\. Off keeps one sky ray every two frames\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveSkyFilterRadius|int|1|0|2|Averages each froxel's sky visibility with its neighbors on screen, this many froxels to each side, before the haze is integrated\. Sky light in the air changes slowly across the screen, so this removes blotches in shaded haze without softening sun shafts, which are not filtered\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveSkyHistoryFrames|int|128|1|255|How many frames of history the near\-field haze averages for how much sky each froxel sees\. The sky light reaching shaded air through a doorway or a gap is a small share of rare ray hits, so it needs a longer average than the sun to stop blotches in dark, hazy areas\. More frames look smoother, but opening a door or moving a large occluder takes longer to show in the haze\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerialPerspectiveUpsampleDepthTolerance|float|0.1|0|1|Stops the near\-field haze of a nearby wall or object from bleeding about 8 pixels onto the sky or distant scenery beside its outline\. When the haze grid is stretched to the screen, a grid column whose deepest surface lies more than this share of the pixel's depth in front of the pixel no longer counts for that pixel\. 0\.1 is 10%\. Smaller is sharper along outlines but can show the grid's steps on steep ground\. 0 turns it off\. Needs a host that passes the tile depth to the haze composite\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerosolDensity|float|1.1|||A multiplier on the density of dust and haze particles\. 1 is typical\. Higher values give a hazier, whiter sky and a larger glow around the sun\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.aerosolTint|float3|1, 1, 1|||A per\-channel multiplier on the haze's scattering and absorption together\. 1, 1, 1 leaves the haze as configured\. Lowering a channel makes the haze affect that color less, so 1, 0\.8, 0\.55 gives a warm dust storm\. Weather and game integrations can set it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.airDensity|float|1|||A multiplier on the density of air molecules\. 1 is a clear Earth sky\. Higher values give a deeper blue sky and redder sunsets\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.airTint|float3|1, 1, 1|||A per\-channel multiplier on the air's scattering\. 1, 1, 1 is Earth's air\. Change it for an alien or polluted sky color\. Weather can blend it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.atmosphereThickness|float|100|||The thickness of the atmosphere, in kilometers\. 100 is Earth\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.calendarYear|int|2026|1900|2100|The year of the date the sky is drawn for, used with Day of Year and Time of Day\. The real moon's position and phase, and the stars, follow it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cameraWorldOverride|float3|0, 0, 0|||The camera's world position in the game's own units and axis convention, used as the cloud anchor while Use Camera World Override is on\. A game integration normally sends it every frame\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudAnchorCutKm|float|0.5|||How far, in kilometers, the cloud anchor has to jump in one frame to count as a camera cut, which rebuilds the cloud reflection dome at once\. Teleports and loading into a new area move it further than a player can travel in one frame\. 0 turns the check off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudBaseHeightMeters|float|1300|||The height of the main cloud deck's underside above the ground level, in meters\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudBodyBillowGain|float|0.65|0|1|The strength of each finer billow size relative to the one above it\. 0\.5 lets the large billows dominate, for smooth lobes\. Toward 0\.8, small billows are nearly as strong as large ones, for a cauliflower look\. Changing it rebuilds the cloud shape field\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudBodyBillowSizeKm|float|1.2|0.2|4|The size of the largest billows, in kilometers\. Three finer sizes follow at a half, a quarter and an eighth of it\. Keep the finest above about 0\.1 km\. Changing it rebuilds the cloud shape field\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudBodyBillowStrength|float|2|0|3|How far the billows push the cloud surface in and out, as a multiple of Billow Size\. 0 gives smooth towers\. Changing it rebuilds the cloud shape field\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudBoilSpeed|float|0.002|||How fast cloud detail is sheared downwind, in kilometers per second\. The tops stream downwind while the bases stay put\. 0 turns the shear off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCellSizeKm|float|3.65|||The average size of a cloud cluster, in kilometers, from 0\.5 to 6\. Smaller values give many small clouds, larger values fewer and broader banks\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusBaseHeightMeters|float|9500|0||The height of the cirrus sheet, in metres\. Real cirrus forms from about 6000 to 13000\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusCoverageMean|float|0.6|0|1|The share of the sky the cirrus patches cover, from 0 to 1\. Low values leave a few isolated streaks, high values a sky combed with them\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusEnable|bool|False|||Draws cirrus: thin, streaked ice cloud high above the other layers\. It is a textured sheet rather than a marched volume, so it costs little, and from below it sits behind every other cloud\. It lights up at sunset after the lower clouds have gone dark\. It casts a faint shadow, stronger with a low sun, which Cloud Shadow Strength fades\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusHaloStrength|float|1|0||The pale ring ice crystals draw 22 degrees from the sun or moon, red on its inner edge, with darker sky just inside it\. It shows only where there is cirrus, so broken streaks give a broken ring\. 0 removes it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusNoiseSeed|float|1|||Picks a different pattern of streaks\. Changing it bakes a new map\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusOpticalDepth|float|0.25|0|3|How opaque the streaks are looking straight up\. Thin cirrus is around 0\.1, thick cirrus around 1\. The path through the sheet lengthens toward the horizon, so they grow more opaque there\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusStreakDirection|float|30|0|360|The compass direction the streaks lie along, in degrees\. Real streaks follow the high wind's shear, which can differ from the wind at the lower clouds\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCirrusWindScale|float|2|0||How fast the cirrus drifts, as a multiple of Cloud Wind Speed and along Cloud Wind Direction\. Winds are faster at this height, but the distance makes the drift look slow\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudColor|float3|0.89, 0.92, 1|||The base color of the clouds\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudColumnBaseVariation|float|0.12|||How much cloud bases rise and fall, as a share of the layer depth, from 0 to 0\.4\. 0 gives perfectly flat bases\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudColumnFeather|float|0.35|||How soft the edges of cloud clusters are, from 0\.05 to 1\. Low values give crisp, solid clouds\. High values give soft, wispy edges\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudColumnTopShape|float|0.4|||How cloud tops rise, from 0\.1 to 2\. Low values let thin cluster edges tower too, for blockier clouds\. High values let only dense cores rise, for domed tops and feathered edges\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudColumnTopVariation|float|0.45|||How much cloud tops vary in height, from 0 to 1\. 0 puts all tops at one height, a flat deck\. Higher values give a varied skyline\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCoverageMean|float|0.54|||The average share of the sky covered by cloud, from 0 \(clear\) to 1 \(overcast\)\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCoverageNoiseScale|float|0.00257732|||How large the regions of different coverage are\. Smaller values give larger regions\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCoverageSpread|float|0|||How much the cloud coverage varies across the sky, from 0 \(the same everywhere\) to 1 \(the full range\)\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCrossTileFillShortHistory|bool|True|||With Quarter March Schedule 2 \(Split, exact\), cloud pixels with almost no history \(after a weather switch, a camera cut or a reveal\) fill from the samples around them across the march's 16\-pixel tiles instead of inside their own tile\. Inside one tile that fill is a single flat blur, which shows as square blocks until the history builds up\. Pixels with a history keep the exact schedule's image\. Off keeps every pixel inside its tile\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudCurlStrengthKm|float|0.12|0|1|How far, in kilometers, the detail is swirled sideways at the cloud base\. It tears the base into wind\-torn wisps and keeps the detail from looking stamped on\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDensity|float|20|||How dense and opaque the clouds are\. Higher values give darker, more solid clouds\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDepthMeters|float|2000|||The vertical depth of the main cloud deck, in meters, measured up from its base\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDetailLodBias|float|-3|||The blur bias used while Cloud Detail LOD Mode is on\. Negative values keep more detail, positive values blur further\. It has no effect while the mode is off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDetailLodMode|int|2|||Reads coarser levels of the cloud detail volume where the march's steps are long, which is cheaper on the GPU\. With the default bias only long steps, far from the camera, read a coarser level, and the image barely changes\.<br>0: Off, full detail at every step\.<br>1: Off, the same as 0\.<br>2: On, by step length \(default\)\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDiffuseSunStrength|float|0|0|1|How much sunlight reaches the shaded parts of clouds after passing through the cloud above them, from 0 to 1\. 0 keeps deep shadows under and between clouds\. 1 lights gray, overcast or stormy skies the way thick cloud passes light, neutral gray rather than blue\. The overcast, drizzle and storm weather presets set 1\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDiskAureole|bool|True|||Draws the glow \(aureole\) that forms around the sun and the moons behind thin cloud, where cloud droplets bend light a few degrees off its path\. Behind thin cloud the disc becomes a bright glow, and behind thicker cloud only the glow remains\. Off, the disc is only dimmed\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudDropletDiameter|float|20|5|50|The typical cloud droplet size, in micrometers, from 5 to 50\. 20 is typical of stratocumulus\. Smaller droplets, as in fresh cumulus and fog, give a wider glow around the sun and the moons\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudEmptyPixelSkip|bool|True|||Skips the cloud filter's work on pixels with no cloud this frame or last frame, such as clear sky and terrain under the deck\. The only visible change is that a pixel a cloud has just left clears up to a frame sooner\. Turn it off to compare\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudEnabled|bool|True|||Draws the clouds\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudEvolutionSpeed|float|0.002|||How fast cloud detail rises through each cloud, in kilometers per second, which makes cumulus look like it is building\. It does not move the clouds themselves\. 0 stops the rise\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudFullRateInsideCloud|bool|True|||With Cloud March Rate on Auto, marches every cloud pixel every frame while the camera moves inside a cloud layer \(including a ground fog bank made with layer 2\), and for a second after\. There the filter can keep only a short history, so the quarter rate leaves about one sample per pixel and the cloud looks blotchy in motion\. It costs up to four times the cloud march's GPU time while it is active\. Off keeps Auto's usual rate\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudHexTilingEnable|bool|True|||Hides repetition in the cloud pattern by blending randomly offset, rotated and mirrored copies of it\. Turn it off only to see the plain repeating pattern\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudHistoryDepthTolerance|float|0.1|0|1|How different the distance to the surface behind a cloud pixel may be for the cloud filters to still reuse a sample there, as a share of that distance\. Lower values reject more history next to moving objects, which avoids smearing at the cost of more noise\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2Albedo|float3|1, 1, 1|0, 0, 0|1, 1, 1|The share of light layer 2's particles scatter rather than absorb, per color channel\. 1, 1, 1 is water droplets, which absorb nothing\. Lower values absorb, and because light scatters many times the color shifts far more than the value suggests\. Dust is about 0\.95, 0\.9, 0\.8\. Smoke and soot are lower\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2BaseHeightMeters|float|5500|||The height of cloud layer 2's base above the ground level, in meters\. 0 or slightly below puts a fog bank on the ground\. Ignored while Layer 2 Follows Camera is on\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2CameraOffsetMeters|float|-30|||Layer 2's base relative to the camera while Layer 2 Follows Camera is on, in meters\. Negative values put the base below the eye: \-30 with a depth of 60 puts the camera in the middle of the layer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2CoverageMean|float|0.85|||The share of the area layer 2 covers, from 0 to 1\. The layer is a sheet broken into patches a few kilometers across\. Near 1 they merge into a closed sheet with a few holes\. Independent of the main deck's coverage\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2DensityScale|float|0.65|||Layer 2's density as a multiple of Cloud Density\. At the default cloud density, 1 gives about 200 m of visibility inside a fog bank and 0\.25 about 800 m\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2DepthMeters|float|2000|||The vertical depth of cloud layer 2, in meters\. Use 50\-500 for ground fog or a dust layer, and a few hundred to 2000 for stratus or a high deck\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2Enable|bool|False|||Draws cloud layer 2: a flat layer such as ground fog, stratus, a high deck or dust\. It is lit like the main deck, works at any height \(including on the ground with the camera inside it\) and is cut off by scene geometry\. It shades and is shaded by the main deck when one lies between the other and the sun\. Shadows on the ground and in the air come only from the main deck\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2FollowCamera|bool|False|||Keeps layer 2's base at the camera's height plus Layer 2 Camera Offset instead of at Layer 2 Base Height, for a dust or fog layer the player always stands in, whatever the terrain height\. Only the height follows the camera\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2NoiseSeed|float|1000|||The seed for layer 2's patch layout\. Different seeds give unrelated layouts\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2TypeMean|float|0.6|||How layer 2's density changes with height, from 0 to 1\. 0 is stratus or fog, densest at the base and thinning toward a ragged top\. 1 is cumulus\-like, thin at the base and full by mid\-height, with billowy rather than wispy detail\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLayer2TypeSpread|float|1|||How much layer 2's cloud type varies across the sky, from 0 to 1\. Independent of the main deck\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLightingLodByDistance|bool|True|||Raises Cloud Lighting LOD Threshold with distance: unchanged within 20 km, four times the threshold from 60 km\. Far clouds reuse their lighting more often, which saves GPU time; their billows can look slightly softer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudLightingLodThreshold|float|0.05|||Saves GPU time by reusing the lighting of nearby cloud samples for samples that barely show, deep inside a cloud or far away, from 0 to 0\.25\. 0 lights every sample\. Raise it for speed\. Too high a value can flatten cloud shading\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudMarchEarlyExit|bool|False|||Stops each cloud ray once 3% of the light behind the cloud still gets through, instead of 1%, so thick cloud cores cost fewer steps\. The result is rescaled so an opaque core stays opaque\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudMarchRate|int|0|0|3|How many cloud pixels are marched each frame\. The others are rebuilt from neighbouring samples and the cloud filter's history\.<br>0: Auto, chosen from the render and output resolution: quarter rate at native resolution and mild upscaling with at least 720 render lines, full rate otherwise\.<br>1: Full, every pixel every frame\.<br>2: Quarter, one pixel of each 2x2 block per frame, for a quarter of the cost\. A still view looks the same as at full rate\.<br>3: Sixteenth, one pixel of each 4x4 block per frame, for a sixteenth of the cost\. Noisy and soft in motion\.<br>The quarter and sixteenth rates need Cloud Temporal History Weight above 0\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudMarchRayBudgetMillions|float|0|0||With Cloud March Rate on Auto, the most cloud rays a frame may march, in millions\. Past it Auto marches a sparser pattern \(a quarter, then a sixteenth of the pixels\), so high render resolutions cost no more than this\. Sparser patterns are noisier in motion\. 0 turns the budget off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudMotionVectorCoverage|float|0.5|0|1|The cloud opacity from which a pixel takes the cloud's depth\. The motion vector blends to the cloud's within 0\.15 of this opacity\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudMotionVectors|bool|True|||Enables depth and screen space motion vectors of the clouds in pixels where they hide the surface behind them\. Upscalers, Ray Reconstruction, motion blur and frame generation then move the clouds separately from that surface\.<br>The clouds are reprojected as static, without their drift in the wind\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudMultiScatterOctaves|int|3|||How many orders of light scattering inside the clouds are approximated, from 1 to 4\. 3 is a good balance\. 1 keeps only light scattered once, which makes cloud interiors dark\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudNoiseTileKm|float|12|||How far, in kilometers, the cloud detail pattern runs before it repeats\. Smaller values show repetition more\. Larger values give coarser detail\. 6 to 24 works well\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudPerformancePreset|int|0|0|1|A switch for cloud shortcuts that are faster but look slightly different\.<br>0: Quality\.<br>1: Performance\. Each cloud sample takes its sun shadow from a simpler shadow map\. Fine billow detail is softer, and billow tops look slightly brighter when the sun is low\.<br>The cloud march rate is set separately by Cloud March Rate\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudPhaseG1|float|0.8|||How strongly clouds scatter light forward\. It creates the bright silver lining at the edges of clouds in front of the sun\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudProfilingLog|bool|False|||Writes cloud GPU timings to the log every 120 frames\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudProfilingMode|int|0|||A cloud diagnostic mode\. 0: Normal\. 9: Reference, a slow, exact path tracer for the cloud lighting, for comparing against the normal mode\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudQuarterMarchSchedule|int|2|0|3|How the GPU runs the quarter\-rate cloud march\. It has no effect at other march rates\.<br>0: One pass\.<br>1: Split: three passes \(the march, a rebuild of every pixel, and a march of the few pixels at object edges that no sample covers\), reading neighboring samples across the passes' tiles\. The image is very close to One pass\.<br>2: Split, exact \(default\): the split passes arranged to give exactly the One pass image\.<br>3: Split with full\-precision samples, for diagnosing the difference between 1 and 2\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudRenderRTEnable|bool|True|||Draws the clouds where the camera sees the sky\. Off, the directly visible sky is cloudless and the on\-screen cloud pass is skipped, unless a debug view needs it\. Reflections and indirect light still get their clouds from the reflection dome\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSecondaryLutEnable|bool|True|||Gives reflections and indirect light their clouds from a small sky dome rebuilt every frame instead of marching the clouds per ray\. This saves a lot of GPU time on cloudy skies\. Off, reflections and indirect light see a cloudless sky\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSecondaryLutInterleaveMode|int|2|||How much of the cloud reflection dome is updated each frame\. The rest is at most a few frames old\. The whole dome is still rebuilt when the clouds change noticeably or the camera cuts\.<br>0: All of it every frame\.<br>1: Half\.<br>2: A quarter\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudShadowMapBakeRowsPerFrame|int|32|8|256|Cloud shadow map rebuilds are spread over several frames, this many rows \(out of 256\) per frame\. Lower values make frames cheaper but follow a moving sun or camera more slowly\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudShadowMapEnable|bool|True|||Stores the clouds' shading toward the sun in a shadow map, rebuilt as the sun moves, instead of marching toward the sun for every cloud sample\. It is much faster\. Turn it off only to compare against the slow reference\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudShadowMapMaxLagDegrees|float|0.25|0|10|While the sun moves fast, such as with a sped\-up time cycle, the shadow map is rebuilt faster so it trails the sun by at most this many degrees\. With a slow sun it costs nothing extra\. 0 always uses Bake Rows per Frame\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudShadowMapRebakeDegrees|float|0.1|0|10|How many degrees the sun has to move before the cloud shadow map is rebuilt\. The map is also rebuilt when the camera moves a quarter of its width or the clouds change\. 0 rebuilds it continuously\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudShadowMapWindowKm|float|6|1|50|The half\-width, in kilometers, of the most detailed cloud shadow map level around the camera\. Each further level is about 3\.3 times wider, and the last reaches the horizon\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudShadowStrength|float|1|||How strongly the clouds shade the sunlight on the ground and in the air, from 0 to 1\. 1 is physical and 0 removes cloud shadows\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSkyAmbientCloudOcclusionStrength|float|1|||How much the clouds change the sky light in the air, from 0 to 1: they hide the sky behind them and add their own scattered light\. 1 is physical\. 0 ignores cloud cover and is meant for debugging\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSpatialDenoise|float|0.1|0|1|An edge\-aware blur on the clouds after the temporal filter, for the noise left where there is little history, such as while flying or inside a cloud\. The value is the brightness difference, as a share, that still gets smoothed: 0\.1 is about 10%\. It changes only the displayed image, not the history\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSunOctaveA|float|0.45|0|1|How much less each further order of scattered sunlight is dimmed by the cloud\. Lower values let more light through, for brighter, softer cloud interiors\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSunOctaveB|float|0.55|0|1|How much each further order of scattered sunlight contributes\. Higher values add more scattered light overall\. Keep it at or below Octave A to stay plausible\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSunOctaveC|float|0.5|0|1|How directional each further order of scattered sunlight is\. Lower values spread the later orders more evenly in all directions\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudSunOctaveCount|int|6|1|8|How many orders of sunlight scattering inside the clouds are approximated, from 1 to 8\. Dense clouds \(high Cloud Density\) need more to stay bright inside\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTemporalHistoryWeight|float|0.95|0|0.98|How much of the previous frames the cloud filter keeps while the camera or the clouds move\. 0\.95 averages about 20 frames\. Higher values hide more noise but smear more in motion\. 0 turns the filter off and shows the raw, noisy march\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTemporalInCloudMinFrames|int|6|0|64|The shortest history, in frames, the cloud filter keeps inside a cloud or fog bank while the camera moves\. There the filter shortens its history to stop the in\-cloud glow from trailing, and at speed it could drop to one or two frames, which showed as blotchy noise while moving through cloud and ground fog\. Higher is calmer but trails more\. 0 removes the floor\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTemporalSmoothLagGradient|bool|True|||Inside a cloud the filter decides how much trailing it can afford from how fast the image changes across the screen\. With this on it measures that change on a slightly blurred history and allows up to 64 instead of 16 pixels of trailing on smooth in\-cloud glow, so the measurement's own noise no longer cuts the history short\. Off uses the raw history\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTemporalStableDepth|bool|True|||Inside a cloud or fog bank the cloud filter finds last frame's image at the cloud's average depth, which in\-cloud wobbles by tens of percent from frame to frame and makes the history swim\. With this on, such pixels use a depth averaged over time instead, so the history holds still while the camera moves\. The cloud depth handed to the haze is that steadier one too\. Off uses this frame's depth\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTemporalStillSamples|int|64|1|1024|How many frames each cloud pixel averages while the view and the clouds are still\. Higher values give calmer still images but take longer to settle after a change the filter does not detect\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTypeMean|float|0|||The average cloud type across the sky, from 0 to 1: 0 is stratus, 0\.5 stratocumulus and 1 cumulus\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTypeNoiseScale|float|0.0034|||How large the regions of different cloud type are\. Smaller values give larger regions\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudTypeSpread|float|0.54|||How much the cloud type varies across the sky, from 0 \(the same everywhere\) to 1 \(the full range\)\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudVerticalOffsetWorldUnits|float|0|||Moves the whole cloud field up or down, in game world units along the up axis\. It changes where the clouds sit without changing their size, the ground level or the haze\. Center Layer at Player sets it once to place the main layer around the player\. Center again after changing World Scale or the layer depth\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudViewSamples|int|32|||The base number of march steps through the cloud layer, from 1 to 32, used while Cloud Step Spacing is 0\. Higher values look better and cost more GPU time\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudViewSamplesMax|int|128|||The maximum number of march steps per ray through the main cloud layer, from 2 to 256\. Rays that leave the clouds or become opaque stop earlier\. Lower it to limit the worst\-case GPU cost\. It has no effect while Cloud Step Spacing is 0\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudViewStepKm|float|0.25|||The target spacing of cloud samples, in kilometers, from 0 to 4\. Larger spacing saves GPU time but can lose detail or miss thin clouds\. Max Cloud Samples separately limits the steps per ray\. 0 uses the fixed step count of Cloud View Samples\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudVoxelGridRebakeGranularityKm|float|0.1|||How far, in kilometers, the clouds or the camera have to move before the clouds count as changed\. A change restarts the gradual updates of the reflection dome and the cloud history\. 0 counts any movement\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudVoxelShadowsEnable|bool|True|||Casts cloud shadows from the cloud shadow map, on the air and on the ground\. Off, there are no cloud shadows on either\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudWeatherJumpResetFrames|int|4|0|30|When the weather changes in a frame or two \(a preset switch with no or a very short ease\), the cloud filter keeps only a couple of frames of history for this many frames, so the whole sky takes on the new weather together instead of patch by patch, with stale patches and blocky fill\. A gradual ease does not trigger it\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudWindDirection|float|45|||The direction the clouds drift toward, in degrees \(0 = \+X, 90 = \+Z\)\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.cloudWindSpeed|float|0.02|||How fast the clouds drift, in kilometers per second\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.dayLengthMinutes|float|24|0.01||How many real minutes one full 24\-hour day lasts while the time cycle runs\. 24 gives one minute per in\-game hour\. Raise it for a slower day\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.dayOfYear|int|80|1|365|The day of the year, from 1 to 365, which sets the season\. 80 is the March equinox \(sunrise due east, sunset due west, a 12\-hour day\), 172 the June solstice and 355 the December solstice\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.debugDispatchCloudRender|bool|True|||Diagnostic: runs the on\-screen cloud pass\. It is skipped anyway while the clouds or Cloud Render are off and no debug view is shown\. Turn it off to measure the pass's cost\. The clouds then freeze in place\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.debugDispatchCloudSkyTransmittance|bool|True|||Diagnostic: runs the small per\-frame pass that measures how much the clouds hide the sky\. Turn it off to measure its cost\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.debugDispatchSkyLuts|bool|True|||Diagnostic: rebuilds the sky's lookup tables when the sun or the atmosphere changes\. Turn it off to freeze them and measure their cost\. The sky then stops following the sun\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.debugEnableSkyMissShading|bool|True|||Diagnostic: shades rays that reach the sky\. Turn it off to draw a flat gray sky and measure the cost of sky shading\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.directionalLightRadianceScale|float|1|||A multiplier on the brightness of the sun and moon lights the sky adds to the scene\. Adjust it if sunlight and moonlight on surfaces read too bright or too dim overall\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.flipUpAxis|bool|False|||Flips the world's up axis for the sky\. Turn it on when the sky renders upside down, with the bright zenith below you and the dark ground half overhead\.<br>A sky that looks rotated 90 degrees is a different problem: rtx\.zUp does not match the game\. To tell them apart, stop the time cycle and set a mid\-height sun: a zenith off to one side means rtx\.zUp, a zenith under your feet means this setting\.<br>Flipping the axis also reverses the direction the sun travels across the sky\. Sun Rotation and North Offset cannot undo that, so the sun's arc has to be placed again afterwards\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.fogAlbedo|float|0.9|0|1|The share of the height fog's dimming that is scattered light rather than absorbed light\. Lower values give a darker, smokier fog\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.fogAnisotropy|float|0.3|-0.95|0.95|How strongly the height fog scatters light forward, from \-0\.95 to 0\.95\. Positive values brighten the fog toward the sun and around lamps\. Water\-droplet fog scatters strongly forward, smoke much less\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.fogBaseHeightMeters|float|0|||The height fog's base, in meters above the ground level\. The fog has its full visibility here and thins above it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.fogHeightFalloffMeters|float|100|1||How quickly the height fog thins with height: its density falls by a factor of e every this many meters above the base\. Large values give an even fog\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.fogNearFadeMeters|float|40|0|1000|A clear zone around the camera for ground\-level fog\. The height fog and cloud layer 2 fade in from nothing at the camera to full density at this distance, so the player's surroundings stay readable in thick fog\. It is a readability aid, not physical\. 0 turns it off\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.fogVisibilityMeters|float|0|0||Height fog: the visibility at the fog's base height, in meters, which is the distance at which contrast falls to 2%\. 0 means no fog\. 50 is thick fog, 1000 mist and 10000 light haze\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.groundAlbedo|float|0.3|0|1|The share of light the ground reflects\. Light bounced off the ground brightens the lower sky, lights the ground seen below the horizon and lights cloud bases from below\. Set it to the scene's typical ground: snow lights cloud bases more than forest or sea\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.groundLevelAuto|bool|True|||Measures the ground level from the scene instead of using Ground Level\. Rays cast straight down \(or ground points the game supplies\) build a smoothed map of the ground height around the player, and the ground level is that map under the camera\. Cloud bases, the height fog and layer 2 then keep their heights above the local ground as the player travels, while climbing a building or jumping changes nothing\. Needs ray\-traced scene geometry or ground points from the game\. Without either, Ground Level is used\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.groundLevelWorldUnits|float|0|||The height, in the game's world units along its up axis, that counts as ground level \(altitude zero\) while Ground Level: Auto is off, or before Auto has measured anything\. Cloud heights, the height fog and the sky's altitude are measured from it\. The easiest way to set it is the Set to Here button, used while standing on ground the game treats as sea level\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.groundSmoothingMeters|float|4000|100||The distance, in meters, over which Ground Level: Auto averages the measured ground\. Larger values follow only the broad shape of the land and move the cloud deck less as the player travels\. Smaller values follow hills and valleys more closely\. Changing it starts a fresh measurement\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.hazeWorldScale|float|1|0.001|1000|The size of the haze's world relative to the game's: World Scale for the haze, height fog, sun shafts and local\-light glow alone\. 1 is real size, so the game's terrain is hazed at its true distance\. With World Scale below 1 and this left at 1, the clouds shrink but distant land is not hazed as if it were farther away\. Settings in meters that belong to the haze \(fog distances and heights, the froxel range\) scale with it\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.latitudeDegrees|float|45|-90|90|The observer's latitude in degrees, positive north\. It sets how high the sun climbs and how tilted its path is\. At 0 \(the equator\) the sun passes almost straight overhead\. High latitudes keep it low, with long, shallow sunrises and sunsets\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.lightningColor|float3|0.72, 0.78, 1|||The color of a lightning flash, in linear RGB, for both the cloud glow and the scene light\. Only the hue counts\. The brightness is set by Lightning Luminous Intensity\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.lightningEnable|bool|True|||Turns lightning on or off\. While on, Lightning Strikes per Minute sets how often it strikes, and the storm weather presets raise that rate\. Turn it off to silence lightning everywhere, including the storm presets and the Test Strike button\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.lightningLuminousIntensity|float|2e+08|0||The peak brightness of a lightning flash, in candela\. It drives the glow in the clouds, the glow in the haze and the light on the scene\. The default is realistic: a flash lights the ground a few kilometers away like a dim overcast day, outshines everything at night, and is barely visible against a daylit cloud\. Raise it for a dramatic daytime storm\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.lightningRangeKm|float|15|||The farthest distance from the camera a strike can land, in kilometers, from 1\.5 to 30\. Strikes spread evenly over the area between 1 km and this distance\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.lightningSceneLight|bool|True|||Adds each flash to the scene as a light at the strike\. Off, the flash lights only the clouds and the air\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.lightningStrikesPerMinute|float|0|||The average number of lightning strikes per minute, from 0 to 60\. The gaps between strikes are random, so strikes cluster and pause naturally\. 0 means no automatic strikes\. Test Strike still works\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.mieAbsorption|float3|0.0044, 0.0044, 0.0044|||The haze's base absorption coefficients per color channel, per kilometer\. Real haze absorbs about as much light as it scatters\. Raising this relative to Mie Scattering darkens the haze, and making it differ between channels tints it: this is how dust becomes brown and dim, or smoke gray and dark, rather than only denser\. Scaled by Aerosol Density\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.mieAnisotropy|float|0.8|||How strongly the haze scatters light forward, from \-1 to 1\. It sets the size and brightness of the glow around the sun\. 0\.8 is typical of Earth's haze\. The sun's disc itself is drawn separately, at Sun Size\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.mieScattering|float3|0.003996, 0.003996, 0.003996|||The haze's base scattering coefficients per color channel, per kilometer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon0.angularDiameter|float|0.518|||The moon's size in the sky, in degrees\. 0\.518 is the size of Earth's Moon\. Its total light stays the same at any size, so a bigger disc has a dimmer surface\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon0.color|float3|1, 1, 1|||The moon's surface brightness and color relative to Earth's Moon\. 1, 1, 1 is lunar\. It scales the moon's light and its disc alike\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon0.elevation|float|45|||The moon's height above the horizon, in degrees\. A game integration can set it every frame, which overrides the saved value\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon0.enabled|bool|False|||Draws this extra moon, placed by its elevation and rotation, for a game that drives its own moon\. Turn rtx\.atmosphere\.numos\.realMoonEnabled off when it replaces the real moon\. Its phase, light and earthshine follow from its position and the sun's, like the real moon's\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon0.phase|float|-1|-1|1|The moon's phase as a point in its cycle: 0 new, 0\.25 first quarter, 0\.5 full and 0\.75 last quarter\. A negative value takes the phase from the moon's position relative to the sun, like the real moon\. Set it for a game whose moon follows its own phase calendar\. Its light, earthshine and lit side follow this phase\. A game integration can set it every frame\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon0.rotation|float|90|||The moon's compass direction \(azimuth\), in degrees\. A game integration can set it every frame, which overrides the saved value\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon1.angularDiameter|float|0.518|||The moon's size in the sky, in degrees\. 0\.518 is the size of Earth's Moon\. Its total light stays the same at any size, so a bigger disc has a dimmer surface\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon1.color|float3|1, 1, 1|||The moon's surface brightness and color relative to Earth's Moon\. 1, 1, 1 is lunar\. It scales the moon's light and its disc alike\.|
