+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon3.phase|float|-1|-1|1|The moon's phase as a point in its cycle: 0 new, 0\.25 first quarter, 0\.5 full and 0\.75 last quarter\. A negative value takes the phase from the moon's position relative to the sun, like the real moon\. Set it for a game whose moon follows its own phase calendar\. Its light, earthshine and lit side follow this phase\. A game integration can set it every frame\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moon3.rotation|float|90|||The moon's compass direction \(azimuth\), in degrees\. A game integration can set it every frame, which overrides the saved value\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moonDiscGainStops|float|0|-16|16|Moon Disc Gain, in stops, on top of Moonlight Gain\. 0 adds nothing\. Brightens or dims only the visible moon discs, not the light they give, so a blown\-out moon can be tamed without darkening the moonlit scene\.<br>Like Night Gain, it does nothing by day and fades in through twilight\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.moonlightGainStops|float|0|-16|16|Moonlight Gain, in stops, on top of Night Gain\. 0 adds nothing\. Brightens or dims everything the moons light: their light on the scene, the moonlit sky and clouds, and their discs\. The stars are not changed\. Lower it when a raised Night Gain makes the moonlight too strong next to the stars\.<br>Like Night Gain, it does nothing by day and fades in through twilight\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.multiScatterPhysicalStrength|float|1|||Blends between a simple approximation of light that scatters many times in the air \(0\) and the physical calculation \(1\)\. 1 gives a natural gradient from the zenith to a warm horizon\. Lower values flatten the gradient and turn the horizon bluer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nightGainStops|float|0|0|16|Night Gain, in stops\. 0 is physical\. Brightens all natural night light together \(the moons, the moonlit sky, starlight, airglow\) so the sky, clouds and ground stay consistent\. Star Gain, Moonlight Gain and Moon Disc Gain adjust the stars and the moons on top of it\.<br>Small values, about \+1 to \+3, make up for the eye's dark adaptation, which a display cannot reproduce, so a physical night looks darker on screen than in reality\. Large values, up to \+10 and beyond, balance the night against a game whose own lights are much brighter than physical\.<br>The sun, twilight and lightning are not changed, and the gain fades in through twilight so the sky never brightens after sunset\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nightSkyStars|bool|True|||Draws the real night sky: about 25,700 catalogue stars as points and the fainter stars as the Milky Way, at their real brightness and color\. The stars turn with the date, time and latitude, dim and redden near the horizon, twinkle, and light the clouds and the ground\. Needs the night sky data files\. Turn it off for a game that draws its own stars\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.northOffsetDegrees|float|0|-360|360|Rotates the sun's whole path so that north in the sky lines up with north in the game world\. Adjust it until the sun rises in the direction the game treats as east\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nubis3AdaptiveStepKm|float|0.025|||The smallest cloud march step, in kilometers, from 0 to 0\.2\. Steps start this fine near the camera to resolve close detail and grow with distance\. 0 uses an even step length everywhere\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nubis3ErosionStrength|float|0.58|||How strongly the detail noise carves into the clouds, from 0 to 2\. 0 gives smooth blobs, 1 the standard look and higher values ragged, heavily carved clouds\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nubis3JitterAnimateKm|float|1e+09|0||The distance, in kilometers, beyond which the cloud sampling noise stops changing every frame\. By default it never stops\. Lower values make distant cloud noise hold still on screen instead of shimmering\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nvdfCoverageOffsetKm|float|0.2|||How much the clouds grow or shrink, in kilometers, per unit of coverage change from the coverage the shape field was built for, from 0 to 4\. Higher values make coverage changes grow clouds faster, so they merge sooner at high coverage\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nvdfNominalCoverage|float|0|||The cloud coverage the cloud shape field is built for, from 0 to 1\. 0 \(Auto\) follows the current coverage and rebuilds the field in the background when it changes\. A fixed value is meant for debugging and look tuning\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nvdfProfileDepthKm|float|0.12|||How far into a cloud, in kilometers, its density takes to build up from the edge, from 0\.1 to 3\. Small values give dense, hard\-edged clouds\. Large values give soft, translucent edges\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.nvdfStepScale|float|0.95|||How far the cloud march jumps through empty air, as a share of the distance to the nearest cloud, from 0 to 0\.95\. Higher values save GPU time, especially toward the horizon\. 0 steps evenly\. Lower it if cloud outlines show onion\-like banding\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.ozoneAbsorption|float3|0.00065, 0.001881, 8.5e-05|||The ozone layer's base absorption coefficients per color channel, per kilometer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.ozoneDensity|float|1|||A multiplier on the density of the ozone layer\. 1 is typical\. Ozone gives the twilight sky its blue color\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.ozoneLayerAltitude|float|25|||The height of the ozone layer's densest point, in kilometers\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.ozoneLayerWidth|float|15|||The half\-thickness of the ozone layer, in kilometers\. Together with Ozone Density it sets the total amount of ozone\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.planetRadius|float|6371|||The planet's radius, in kilometers\. 6371 is Earth\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.qualityLevel|int|0|0|2|Scales the GPU cost of the clouds and the haze for slower GPUs\. It works on top of the other quality settings and changes none of them\.<br>0: High, the full quality\.<br>1: Medium\. The clouds march a quarter of their pixels each frame at every resolution, and the near\-field haze uses larger froxels and fewer depth slices\. A still view looks nearly the same; in motion the clouds are slightly softer\.<br>2: Low\. Medium's savings, plus the faster cloud sun shadows of Cloud Performance Preset 1, fewer cloud samples per ray and a coarser haze grid\. Fine billow detail and sun shafts are softer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.radianceAutoScale|bool|True|||Scales everything the sky outputs by a power of two that follows the light level, so a night sky, which is about a hundred\-millionth as bright as the day sky, keeps its precision in 16\-bit render targets\. Daylight always uses a scale of 1\. Turn it off only for debugging: a moonless night then turns black in 16\-bit targets\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.rayleighScattering|float3|0.005802, 0.013558, 0.0331|||The air's base scattering coefficients per color channel, per kilometer\. They give the sky its blue color\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.realMoonAngularDiameter|float|0.518|0.05|20|The drawn size of the real moon, in degrees\. 0\.518 is the Moon's real size\. A larger disc gives the same total moonlight, so its surface looks dimmer\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.realMoonEnabled|bool|True|||Draws Earth's Moon where it really is for the configured date, time and latitude\. Its phase, brightness, earthshine and eclipses follow from where the sun is\. It lights the sky, the clouds and the ground\. Turn it off for a game that places its own moon with rtx\.atmosphere\.numos\.moon0\-3\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.skyIndirectRadianceScale|float|1|0||A multiplier on the sky light that reaches surfaces through diffuse bounces\. 1 is physical\. Raise it when shaded areas look too dull next to sunlit ones\. Reflections and the visible sky keep their physical brightness, so reflections still match the sky\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.skyLutCacheKeySplitEnable|bool|True|||Recalculates each part of the sky only when its own inputs change, so a moving sun updates only the small sky view table\. The image is unchanged\. Turn it off only to compare against recalculating everything together\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.skyViewAltitudeRebakeGranularityKm|float|0.05|||How far, in kilometers, the camera has to climb or descend before the sky is recalculated for the new height\. The default is 0\.05 \(50 m\)\. 0 never recalculates for height, so the sky stays as seen from where it was last calculated\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.skyViewRebakeGranularityDeg|float|0.1|||How many degrees the sun or a moon has to move before the sky is recalculated\. 0\.1 follows the sun smoothly at a small cost\. 0 recalculates every frame while the sun moves\. Other sky changes always recalculate at once\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.starGainStops|float|0|-16|16|Star Gain, in stops, on top of Night Gain\. 0 adds nothing\. Brightens or dims the stars and the Milky Way, and the starlight on the sky, clouds and ground, without changing the moons\. Raise it to show more stars on a moonlit night or under a game whose own lights are bright\.<br>Like Night Gain, it does nothing by day and fades in through twilight\.|
+|rtx.atmosphere.numos.starScintillation|float|1|0|4|How strongly the stars twinkle, relative to a typical night \(1\)\. Stars near the horizon twinkle most and can flash colors, while high stars stay steady\. 0 turns twinkling off\.|
